== Conviction of the Reality of Divine Things

V. Truly gracious affections are accompanied by a reasonable and spiritual conviction of the judgment, of the reality and certainty of divine things.

This seems to be implied in the text that was laid as the foundation of this discourse: “Though you#super[+] have not seen Him, you#super[+] love Him; and though you#super[+] do not see Him now, you#super[+] #emph[believe] in Him and rejoice with joy unspeakable and full of glory.”

All who are truly gracious persons have a solid, full, thorough and effectual conviction of the truth of the great things of the gospel. I mean that they no longer waver between two opinions. The great doctrines of the gospel are no longer doubtful things to them, or matters of opinion which, though probable, are still open to dispute. With them they are settled and determined points, undoubted and indisputable, so that they are not afraid to venture their all on their truth. Their conviction is an #emph[effectual] conviction, so that the great, spiritual, mysterious and invisible things of the gospel have the #emph[influence] of real and certain things on them. They have the #emph[weight] and #emph[power] of real things in their hearts, and so they rule in their affections and govern them through the course of their lives. As to Christ’s being the Son of God and Savior of the world, and the great things he has revealed about himself, his Father and another world, they do not merely hold a prevailing opinion that these things are true, and so give their assent, as they do in many other matters of doubtful speculation. They #emph[see that it is really so:] their eyes are opened, so that they see that Jesus really is the Christ, the Son of the living God. And as to the things Christ has revealed of God’s eternal purposes and designs concerning fallen man, and the glorious and everlasting things prepared for the saints in another world, they see that these things are indeed so. Therefore these things carry great weight with them, and have a mighty power on their hearts and influence over their practice, in some measure answerable to their infinite importance.

That all true Christians have this kind of conviction of the truth of the things of the gospel is abundantly plain from the holy Scriptures. I will mention a few places out of many. Matt 16:15–17: “Who do you#super[+] say I am?” Simon Peter answered, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.” Jesus replied, “Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! … this was … revealed to you … by My Father in heaven.” John 6:68–69: “You have the words of eternal life. We believe and know that You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.” John 17:6–8: “I have manifested Your name to those You have given Me out of the world. … Now they know that everything You have given Me comes from You. For I have given them the words You gave Me, and they have received them. They knew with certainty that I came from You, and they believed that You sent Me.” Acts 8:37: “If you believe with all your heart, you may.” 2 Cor 4:11–14: “For we who are alive are always consigned to death for Jesus’ sake … death is at work in us … And in keeping with what is written: ‘I believed, therefore I have spoken,’ we who have the same spirit of faith also believe and therefore speak, knowing that the One who raised the Lord Jesus will also raise us with Jesus and present us with you#super[+] in His presence.” Together with verse 16: “Therefore we do not lose heart.” And verse 18: “We fix our eyes not on what is seen,” and so on. And 5:1: “Now we know that if the earthly tent we live in is dismantled, we have a building from God,” and verses 6–8: “Therefore we are always confident, although we know that while we are at home in the body, we are away from the Lord. For we walk by faith, not by sight. We are confident, then, and would prefer to be away from the body and at home with the Lord.” 2 Tim 1:12: “For this reason, even though I suffer as I do, I am not ashamed; for I know whom I have believed, and I am convinced that He is able to guard what I have entrusted to Him for that day.” Heb 3:6: “And we are His house, if we hold firmly to our confidence and the hope of which we boast.” Heb 11:1: “Now faith is the substance of what we hope for and the evidence of what we do not see,” together with that whole chapter. 1 John 4:13–16: “By this we know that we remain in Him, and He in us: He has given us of His Spirit. And we have seen and testify that the Father has sent His Son to be the Savior of the world. If anyone confesses that Jesus is the Son of God, God abides in him, and he in God. And we have come to know and believe the love that God has for us.” Chapter 5:4–5: “For everyone born of God overcomes the world. And this is the victory that has overcome the world: our faith. Who then overcomes the world? Only he who believes that Jesus is the Son of God.”

Truly gracious affections, therefore, are accompanied by the kind of conviction and persuasion of the truth of the things of the gospel, and sight of their evidence and reality, that these and other Scriptures speak of.

There are many religious affections that are not accompanied by such a conviction of the judgment. Some people have many apprehensions and ideas which they call divine discoveries, which are affecting but not convincing. For a little while they may seem more persuaded of the truth of the things of religion than they used to be, and may give a ready assent, like many of Christ’s hearers who believed for a while. Yet they have no thorough and effectual conviction. Nor is there any great and lasting change in them in this respect: that whereas before they did not take the great things of the gospel as real, now these things appear new to them in their reality and certainty, and they see them in quite another light than they used to. There are many persons who have been exceedingly raised with religious affections, and think they have been converted, who do not go about the world any more convinced of the truth of the gospel than they used to be; or at least there is no remarkable change. They are not men who live under the influence and power of a conviction that makes real to them the infinite and eternal things the gospel reveals; if they were, it would be impossible for them to live as they do. Because their affections are not accompanied by a thorough conviction of the mind, they are not at all to be depended on. However great a show and noise they make, it is like the blaze of tow, or the crackling of thorns, or like the forward, flourishing blade on stony ground, which has no root, nor depth of earth to keep it alive.

Some persons, under high affections and a confident persuasion of their good state, have something which they very ignorantly call seeing the truth of the word of God, though it is very far from it. It happens like this. Some text of Scripture comes to their minds in a sudden and extraordinary way, declaring to them immediately (as they suppose) that their sins are forgiven, or that God loves them and will save them. Perhaps a chain of Scriptures comes one after another to the same purpose. And they are convinced that it is truth; that is, they are confident that it is certainly so, that their sins are forgiven and God does love them, and so on. They say they know it is so. And when the words of Scripture are suggested to them in this meaning, and, as they suppose, spoken to them immediately by God, they are ready to cry out, “Truth, truth! It is certainly so! The word of God is true!” And this they call seeing the truth of the word of God. Yet the whole of their faith amounts to no more than a strong confidence of their own good state, and so a confidence that those words are true which they suppose tell them they are in a good state. But in fact (as was shown before) there is no Scripture that declares any person to be in a good state directly, or in any other way than by consequence. So this, instead of being a real sight of the word of God, is a sight of nothing but a phantom, and is a delusion through and through. Truly to see the truth of the word of God is to see the truth of the gospel: the glorious doctrine the word of God contains about God and Jesus Christ, and the way of salvation by him, and the world of glory that he has entered into and purchased for all who believe. It is not a revelation that such and such particular persons are true Christians and will go to heaven. Therefore those affections which arise from no other persuasion of the truth of the word of God than this arise from delusion, not true conviction, and so are themselves delusive and vain.

But even if the religious affections persons have do indeed arise from a strong persuasion of the truth of the Christian religion, their affections are no better for it unless their persuasion is a #emph[reasonable] persuasion or conviction. By a reasonable conviction I mean a conviction founded on real evidence, or on what is a good reason or just ground of conviction. Men may have a strong persuasion that the Christian religion is true when their persuasion is not built on evidence at all, but entirely on upbringing and the opinion of others; just as many #emph[Muslims] are strongly persuaded of the truth of the #emph[Muslim] religion because their fathers, their neighbors and their nation believe it. A belief in the truth of the Christian religion that is built on the very same grounds as the #emph[Muslims’] belief in the #emph[Muslim] religion is the same sort of belief. And though the thing believed happens to be better, that does not make the belief itself of a better sort. For though the thing believed happens to be true, the belief of it is not owing to its truth but to upbringing. So as the conviction is no better than the #emph[Muslims’] conviction, so the affections that flow from it are no better in themselves than the religious affections of #emph[Muslims.]

But if the belief in Christian doctrines from which persons’ affections arise is not merely from upbringing, but really from reasons and arguments that are offered, it does not necessarily follow from this that their affections are truly gracious. For that, it is required that the belief from which their affections arise should be not only a #emph[reasonable] but also a #emph[spiritual] belief or conviction. I suppose no one will doubt that some natural men give a kind of assent of their judgment to the truth of the Christian religion from the rational proofs or arguments offered to demonstrate it. #emph[Judas] no doubt thought #emph[Jesus] to be the #emph[Messiah] from the things he saw and heard; yet all along he was a devil. So in John 2:23–25 we read of #emph[many who believed in Christ’s name when they saw the miracles he did,] whom Christ nonetheless knew had nothing within them that could be depended on. So #emph[Simon the sorcerer] believed when he saw the miracles and signs that were done, yet remained “poisoned by bitterness and captive to iniquity” (Acts 8:13, 23). And if there is such a belief or assent of the judgment in some natural men, no one can doubt that religious affections may arise from that assent or belief; as we read of some who #emph[believed for a while,] who were greatly affected, and #emph[at once received the word with joy.]

It is evident that there is such a thing as a #emph[spiritual] belief or conviction of the truth of the things of the gospel: a belief that is peculiar to those who are spiritual, who are regenerated and have the Spirit of God in his holy communications, dwelling in them as a vital principle. So the conviction they have does not differ from that of natural men only in what goes along with it, in that it is accompanied by good works. The belief itself is different: the assent and conviction of the judgment is of a kind peculiar to those who are spiritual, a kind which natural men are wholly without. This is evident from Scripture, if anything at all is. John 17:8: “They believed that You sent Me.” Titus 1:1: “for the faith of God’s elect and their knowledge of the truth that leads to godliness.” John 16:27: “For the Father Himself loves you#super[+], because you#super[+] have loved Me and have believed that I came from God.” 1 John 4:15: “If anyone confesses that Jesus is the Son of God, God abides in him, and he in God.” Chapter 5:1: “Everyone who believes that Jesus is the Christ has been born of God.” Verse 10: “Whoever believes in the Son of God has this testimony within him.”

What a #emph[spiritual conviction] of the judgment is, we are naturally led to determine from what has already been said under the former head of a #emph[spiritual understanding.] The conviction of the judgment arises from the illumination of the understanding: passing a right judgment on things depends on having a right apprehension or idea of them. And so it follows that a spiritual conviction of the truth of the great things of the gospel is a conviction that arises from having a spiritual view or apprehension of those things in the mind. This is also evident from Scripture, which often represents a saving belief of the reality and divinity of the things set before us and exhibited in the gospel as coming from the Spirit of God enlightening the mind to have right apprehensions of the nature of those things; and so, as it were, unveiling them or revealing them, and enabling the mind to view them and see them as they are. Luke 10:21–22: “I praise You, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because You have #emph[hidden] these things from the wise and learned, and #emph[revealed] them to little children. Yes, Father, for this was well-pleasing in Your sight. All things have been entrusted to Me by My Father. No one knows who the Son is except the Father, and no one knows who the Father is except the Son and those to whom the Son chooses to #emph[reveal] Him.” John 6:40: “For it is My Father’s will that everyone who #emph[sees] the Son and #emph[believes] in Him shall have eternal life.” Here it is plain that true faith arises from a spiritual sight of Christ. And John 17:6–8: “I have #emph[manifested] Your name to those You have given Me out of the world. … Now they know that everything You have given Me comes from You. For I have given them the words You gave Me, and they have received them. They knew with certainty that I came from You, and they believed that You sent Me.” Here Christ’s manifesting God’s name to the disciples, or giving them a true apprehension and view of divine things, was that by which they knew that Christ’s doctrine was of God, and that Christ himself was from him and was sent by him. Matt 16:16–17: Simon Peter said, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.” Jesus replied, “Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For this was not #emph[revealed] to you by flesh and blood, but by My Father in heaven.” 1 John 5:10: “Whoever believes in the Son of God has this testimony within him.” Gal 1:14–16: “… extremely zealous for the traditions of my fathers. But when God, who set me apart from my mother’s womb and called me by His grace, was pleased to #emph[reveal] His Son in me so that I might preach Him among the Gentiles, I did not rush to consult with flesh and blood.”

If, then, a spiritual conviction of the divinity and reality of the things exhibited in the gospel is one that arises from a spiritual understanding of those things, I have already shown what that is: namely, a sense and taste of the divine, supreme and holy excellence and beauty of those things. So the mind is spiritually convinced of the divinity and truth of the great things of the gospel when that conviction arises, either directly or remotely, from such a sense or view of their divine excellence and glory as is exhibited there. This clearly follows from what has already been said, and on this the Scripture is very plain and explicit. 2 Cor 4:3–6: “And even if our gospel is veiled, it is veiled to those who are perishing. The god of this age has blinded the minds of #emph[unbelievers] so they cannot see the light of the gospel of the #emph[glory] of Christ, who is the image of God. For we do not proclaim ourselves, but Jesus Christ as Lord, and ourselves as your#super[+] servants for Jesus’ sake. For God, who said, ‘Let light shine out of darkness,’ made His light shine in our hearts to give us the #emph[light of the knowledge of the glory of God] in the face of Jesus Christ.” Together with the last verse of the chapter before, which introduces this: “And we, who with unveiled faces all behold the #emph[glory of the Lord,] are being transformed into His image with intensifying glory, which comes from the Lord, who is the Spirit.” Nothing can be more evident than that the apostle here speaks of a saving belief of the gospel as arising from the mind’s being enlightened to behold the divine glory of the things the gospel exhibits.

This view or sense of the divine glory and unparalleled beauty of the things exhibited to us in the gospel tends to convince the mind of their divinity in two ways: #emph[directly,] and more #emph[indirectly] and #emph[remotely.] 1. A view of this divine glory convinces the mind #emph[directly] of the divinity of these things, since this glory is in itself a direct, clear and all-conquering evidence of it; especially when it is clearly shown, or when this supernatural sense is given in a good degree.

He who has his judgment #emph[directly] convinced and assured in this way of the divinity of the things of the gospel, by a clear view of their divine glory, has a #emph[reasonable] conviction. His belief and assurance is entirely agreeable to reason, because the divine glory and beauty of divine things is in itself real evidence of their divinity, and the most direct and strong evidence. He who truly sees the divine, transcendent, supreme glory of those things that are divine does, as it were, know their divinity intuitively. He does not only argue that they are divine; he sees that they are divine. He sees in them that in which divinity chiefly consists. For it is in this glory, so vastly and inexpressibly different from the glory of man-made things and from all other glory, that the true notion of divinity mainly consists. God is God, and distinguished from all other beings and exalted above them, chiefly by his divine beauty, which is infinitely different from all other beauty. Those, therefore, who see the stamp of this glory in divine things see divinity in them; they see God in them, and so see them to be divine, because they see in them that in which the truest idea of divinity consists. In this way a soul may have a kind of intuitive knowledge of the divinity of the things exhibited in the gospel. This is not to say that he judges the doctrines of the gospel to be from God without any argument or deduction at all; but it is without any long chain of arguments. There is only one argument, and the evidence is direct: the mind rises to the truth of the gospel by only one step, and that is its divine glory.

It would be very strange if any professing Christian should deny that it is possible for there to be an excellence in divine things so transcendent, and so exceedingly different from what is in other things, that if it were seen it would clearly distinguish them. We cannot rationally doubt that things which are #emph[divine,] which belong to the Supreme Being, are vastly different from things that are #emph[human;] that there is a godlike, high and glorious excellence in them which so distinguishes them from the things of men that the difference is beyond words; and therefore an excellence such that, if it is seen, it will have a most convincing and satisfying influence on anyone that they are what they are, namely, divine. No doubt there is such glory and excellence in the divine Being, by which he is so infinitely distinguished from all other beings, that if it were seen he might be known by it. It would therefore be very unreasonable to deny that it is possible for God to give manifestations of this distinguishing excellence in the things by which he is pleased to make himself known, and that this distinguishing excellence may be clearly seen in them. There are #emph[natural] excellences that very evidently distinguish their subjects or authors to anyone who beholds them. How different the speech of an intelligent man is from that of a little child! And how greatly the speech of some men of great genius, such as #emph[Homer, Cicero, Milton, Locke, Addison] and others, is distinguished from that of many other intelligent men! There are no limits to be set to the degrees in which mental excellence may be shown in speech. But the appearances of the #emph[natural] perfections of God, in the manifestations he makes of himself, may no doubt distinguish him unspeakably more clearly than the appearances of those excellences of worms of the dust by which they differ from one another. One who is well acquainted with mankind and their works may know, by looking at the sun, that it is no human work. And it is reasonable to suppose that when Christ comes at the end of the world in the glory of his Father, it will be with such indescribable appearances of divinity as will leave no doubt in the inhabitants of the world, even the most obstinate unbelievers, that he who appears is a divine person. But above all, the manifestations of the #emph[moral] and #emph[spiritual] glory of the divine Being (which is the proper beauty of the divinity) bring their own evidence and tend to assure the heart. So the disciples were assured that Jesus was the Son of God, for #emph[they beheld his glory, the glory of the one and only Son from the Father, full of grace and truth] (John 1:14). When Christ appeared to his disciples in the glory of his transfiguration, with that outward glory to their bodily eyes, which was a sweet and wonderful symbol and likeness of his spiritual glory, together with his spiritual glory itself manifested to their minds, the manifestation of glory was such as perfectly, and with good reason, assured them of his divinity. This appears from what one of them, namely the apostle #emph[Peter,] says about it, 2 Pet 1:16–18: “For we did not follow cleverly devised fables when we made known to you#super[+] the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, but we were eyewitnesses of His majesty. For He received honor and glory from God the Father when the voice came to Him from the Majestic Glory, saying, ‘This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.’ And we ourselves heard this voice from heaven when we were with Him on the holy mountain.” The apostle calls that mountain #emph[the holy mountain] because the manifestation of Christ made there to their minds, with which their minds were especially impressed and ravished, was the glory of his #emph[holiness,] or the beauty of his #emph[moral excellence;] or, as another of these disciples who saw it expresses it, #emph[his glory,] as #emph[full of grace and truth.]

Now this distinguishing glory of the divine Being has its brightest appearance and manifestation in the things set before us and exhibited in the gospel: the doctrines taught there, the word spoken there, and the divine counsels, acts and works revealed there. These things contain the clearest, most wonderful and distinguishing representations and exhibitions of the glory of God’s moral perfections that were ever made to the world. And if there is such a distinguishing, evidential manifestation of divine glory in the gospel, it is reasonable to suppose that there may be such a thing as seeing it. What should keep it from being seen? That some do not see it, though they may be discerning men in temporal matters, is no argument that it cannot be seen. If there are such indescribable, distinguishing, evidential excellences in the gospel, it is reasonable to suppose that they are such as cannot be discerned except by the special influence and enlightening of the Spirit of God. Uncommon strength of mind is needed to discern the distinguishing excellences of the works of authors of great genius: those things in #emph[Milton] which appear tasteless, and even imperfections, to poor judges are his inimitable excellences in the eyes of those who have greater discernment and better taste. And if there is a book of which God is the author, it is most reasonable to suppose that the distinguishing glories of his word are of such a kind that the sin and corruption of men’s hearts would blind men from discerning the beauties of such a book. For that sin and corruption, above all things, alienates men from the Deity, and makes the heart dull and insensible to any sense or taste of those things in which the moral glory of the divine perfections consists. I say, it is only reasonable to suppose this, and that therefore they will not see those beauties except as God is pleased to enlighten them and restore a holy taste, to discern and relish divine beauties.

This sense of the spiritual excellence and beauty of divine things also tends #emph[directly] to convince the mind of the truth of the gospel, because very many of the most important things declared in the gospel, which are hidden from the eyes of natural men, have a truth that in effect consists in this excellence, or depends so immediately on it and results from it, that when this excellence is seen, the truth of those things is seen. As soon as the eyes are opened to behold the holy beauty and loveliness that is in divine things, a multitude of the most important doctrines of the gospel that depend on it (all of which seem strange and dark to natural men) are seen at once to be true. For instance, in this way the truth of what the word of God declares about the exceeding evil of sin appears. For the same eye that discerns the transcendent beauty of holiness necessarily sees in it the exceeding odiousness of sin; the same taste that relishes the sweetness of true moral good tastes the bitterness of moral evil. And by this means a man sees his own sinfulness and loathsomeness, for he now has a sense to discern objects of this nature. So he sees the truth of what the word of God declares about the exceeding sinfulness of mankind, which he did not see before. He now sees the dreadful pollution of his heart and the desperate depravity of his nature in a new way, for his soul has now been given a sense to feel the pain of such a disease. And this shows him the truth of what the Scripture reveals about the corruption of man’s nature, his original sin, and the ruined, undone condition man is in, and his need of a Savior, his need of the mighty power of God to renew his heart and change his nature. By seeing the true excellence of holiness, men see the glory of all those things which both reason and Scripture show to be in the divine Being; for it has been shown that their glory depends on this. And in this way they see the truth of all that the Scripture declares about God’s glorious excellence and majesty, his being the fountain of all good, the only happiness of the creature, and so on. And this again shows the mind the truth of what the Scripture teaches about the evil of sin against so glorious a God; and also the truth of what it teaches about sin’s just desert of the dreadful punishment it reveals; and also about the impossibility of our offering any satisfaction or sufficient atonement for what is so infinitely evil and heinous. And this again shows the truth of what the Scripture reveals about the necessity of a Savior, to offer an atonement of infinite value for sin. And this sense of spiritual beauty that has been spoken of enables the soul to see the glory of those things the gospel reveals about the person of Christ. So it enables the soul to see the exceeding beauty and dignity of his person, appearing in what the gospel exhibits of his word, works, acts and life. And this apprehension of the supreme dignity of his person shows the truth of what the gospel declares about the value of his blood and righteousness; and so the infinite excellence of the offering he has made to God for us; and so its sufficiency to atone for our sins and to commend us to God. In this way the Spirit of God shows the way of salvation by Christ. In this way the soul sees the fitness and suitableness of this way of salvation, the wonderful wisdom of its design, and how perfectly the provision the gospel exhibits (as made for us) answers to our needs. When a sense of true divine beauty is given to the soul, the soul discerns the beauty of every part of the gospel scheme. This also shows the soul the truth of what the word of God declares about man’s chief happiness, as consisting in holy exercises and enjoyments. This shows the truth of what the gospel declares about the unspeakable glory of the heavenly state. And what the prophecies of the Old Testament and the writings of the apostles declare about the glory of the Messiah’s kingdom is now all plain; and also what the Scripture teaches about the reasons and grounds of our duty. The truth of all these things revealed in the Scripture, and many more that might be mentioned, appears to the soul only by the imparting of that spiritual taste of divine beauty which has been spoken of. They were hidden things to the soul before.

And besides all this, the truth of all those things the Scripture says about experiential religion is known in this way, for they are now experienced. And this convinces the soul that one who knew the heart of man better than we know our own hearts, and knew perfectly the nature of virtue and holiness, was the author of the Scriptures. And the opening to view, with such clearness, of such a world of wonderful and glorious truth in the gospel, which was unknown before, being quite beyond the sight of a natural eye but now appearing so clear and bright, has a powerful and invincible influence on the soul to persuade it of the divinity of the gospel.

Unless men may come to a reasonable, solid persuasion and conviction of the truth of the gospel by its internal evidences, in the way that has been spoken of, namely by a sight of its glory, it is impossible that those who are unlearned and unacquainted with history should have any thorough and effectual conviction of it at all. Without this they may see a great deal of probability in it. It may be reasonable for them to give much credit to what learned men and historians tell them; and these may tell them so much that it may look very probable and rational to them that the Christian religion is true, so much that they would be very unreasonable not to hold this opinion. But to have a conviction so clear, evident and assuring as to be enough to move them boldly to sell all, to run confidently and fearlessly the risk of losing all things and of enduring the most exquisite and long-continued torments, and to trample the world underfoot and count all things as rubbish for Christ: for this, the evidence they can have from history cannot be enough. It is impossible that men who do not have something of a general view of the historical world, or the course of history from age to age, should grasp the force of arguments for the truth of Christianity drawn from history to such a degree as effectually to move them to venture their all on it. After all that learned men have said to them, countless doubts will remain in their minds. When pressed by some great trial of their faith, they will be ready to say, “How do I know this, or that? How do I know when these histories were written? Learned men tell me these histories were attested in such and such a way in their own day; but how do I know that there were such attestations then? They tell me there is as much reason to believe these facts as any whatever that are related at such a distance; but how do I know that the other facts related of those ages ever happened?” Those who do not have something of a general view of the course of historical events, and of the state of mankind from age to age, cannot see the clear evidence from history of the truth of facts in distant ages; endless doubts and scruples will remain.

But the gospel was not given only for learned men. At least nineteen in twenty, if not ninety-nine in a hundred, of those for whom the Scriptures were written are not capable of any certain or effectual conviction of the divine authority of the Scriptures by such arguments as learned men use. If men who have been brought up in heathenism must wait for a clear and certain conviction of the truth of Christianity until they have learning and acquaintance with the histories of more cultivated nations, enough to see clearly the force of such arguments, it will make the evidence of the gospel immensely cumbersome to them, and will make the spreading of the gospel among them infinitely difficult. Miserable is the condition of the #emph[Houssatunnuck Indians,] and others who have lately shown a desire to be instructed in Christianity, if they can come at no evidence of the truth of Christianity sufficient to move them to sell all for Christ in any other way than this.

It is unreasonable to suppose that God has provided his people with no more than probable evidences of the truth of the gospel. With great care he has abundantly provided and given them the most convincing, assuring, satisfying and manifold evidence of his faithfulness in the covenant of grace; and, as #emph[David] says, he has made a covenant “ordered and secured in every part.” It is therefore rational to suppose that at the same time he would not fail to order the matter so that there would be no lack of evidence just as great and clear #emph[that this is his covenant,] and that these promises are his promises; or, what is the same thing, that the Christian religion is true and that the gospel is his word. Otherwise the great assurances he has given of his faithfulness in his covenant, by confirming it with his oath and establishing it in so many ways by seals and pledges, are in vain. For the evidence that it is his covenant is, properly, the foundation on which all the force and effect of those other assurances stand. We may therefore suppose and conclude without doubt that God has given some sort of evidence, beyond all mere probability, that this covenant and these promises are his: that there are some grounds of assurance of it held out, of which the unlearned and those unacquainted with history are capable, and which, if we are not blind to them, tend to give a higher persuasion than any arguing from history, human tradition and the like. Indeed, God has given what is good ground for the highest and most perfect assurance that mankind have in any case whatever; agreeable to those high expressions the apostle uses, Heb 10:22: “Let us draw near … in #emph[full] assurance of faith.” And Col 2:2: “that they may be encouraged in heart, knit together in love, and filled with #emph[all the riches of the full assurance of understanding,] so that they may know the mystery of God, namely Christ.” It is reasonable to suppose that God would give the greatest evidence of those things which are greatest, and whose truth is of greatest importance to us; things of which we, therefore, if we are wise and act rationally, will have the greatest desire to have full, undoubting and perfect assurance. But it is certain that such an assurance cannot be attained by the greater part of those who live under the gospel by arguments drawn from ancient traditions, histories and monuments.

And if we come to fact and experience, there is not the least reason to suppose that one in a hundred of those who have been sincere Christians, and have had a heart to sell all for Christ, came by their conviction of the truth of the gospel in this way. If we read through the histories of the many thousands who have died as martyrs for Christ since the beginning of the Reformation, and have cheerfully undergone extreme tortures in confidence of the truth of the gospel, and consider their circumstances and advantages, how few of them can we reasonably suppose ever came by their assured persuasion in this way, or indeed could possibly have received so full and strong an assurance reasonably from such arguments! Many of them were weak women and children, and the greater part of them unlearned persons. Many of these had been brought up in popish ignorance and darkness and had only newly come out of it, and they lived and died in times when those arguments for the truth of Christianity from antiquity and history had been handled only very imperfectly. And indeed, it is only very lately that these arguments have been set in a clear and convincing light, even by learned men themselves. And since that has been done, there have never been fewer thorough believers among those who have been brought up in the true religion: unbelief never prevailed so much in any age as in this one, in which these arguments are handled to the greatest advantage.

The true martyrs of Jesus Christ are not those who have only been strong in the opinion that the gospel of Christ is true, but #emph[those who have seen the truth of it;] as the very name of martyrs, or witnesses (by which they are called in Scripture), implies. Those who only declare that they are very much of the opinion that something is true are very improperly called witnesses of its truth. Only those are proper witnesses who can and do testify that they have seen the truth of the thing they assert. John 3:11: “We speak of what we know, and we #emph[testify] to what we have seen.” John 1:34: “I have #emph[seen] and #emph[testified] that this is the Son of God.” 1 John 4:14: “And we have seen and #emph[testify] that the Father has sent His Son to be the Savior of the world.” Acts 22:14–15: “The God of our fathers has appointed you to #emph[know] His will, and to #emph[see] the Righteous One, and to #emph[hear] His voice. You will be His #emph[witness] to everyone of what you have #emph[seen] and #emph[heard.]” Now the true martyrs of Jesus Christ are called his witnesses; and all the saints who, by their holy practice under great trials, declare that faith which is “the #emph[substance] of what we hope for and the #emph[evidence] of what we do not see” are called witnesses (Heb 11:1 and 12:1). For by their profession and practice they declare their assurance of the truth and divinity of the gospel, having had the eyes of their minds enlightened to see divinity in the gospel, or to behold that unparalleled, indescribably excellent and truly divine glory shining in it, which is altogether distinguishing, evidential and convincing. So they may truly be said to have seen God in it, and to have seen that it is indeed divine; and so they can speak in the manner of witnesses. They do not only say that #emph[they think] the gospel is divine, but say that #emph[it is divine,] giving this as their testimony, because they have seen it to be so. No doubt #emph[Peter, James] and #emph[John,] after they had seen that excellent glory of Christ on the mountain, would have been ready when they came down to speak in the language of witnesses, and to say positively that #emph[Jesus is the Son of God;] as #emph[Peter] says, they were “eyewitnesses” (2 Pet 1:16). And so all nations will be ready to say this positively when they behold his glory at the day of judgment; though what will be seen by all will be only his natural glory, not his moral and spiritual glory, which is much more distinguishing. But it must still be noted that among those who have a spiritual sight of the divine glory of the gospel there is a great variety of degrees of strength of faith, as there is a vast variety in the degrees of clearness of their views of this glory. But there is no true and saving faith, or spiritual conviction of the judgment of the truth of the gospel, that has nothing in it of this manifestation of its internal evidence in some degree. The gospel of the blessed God does not go abroad begging for its evidence as much as some think; it has its highest and most proper evidence in itself. Great use may be made of external arguments; they are not to be neglected, but highly prized and valued, for they may be of great service to awaken unbelievers and bring them to serious consideration, and to confirm the faith of true saints. Indeed, they may in some respects serve #emph[toward the begetting] of a saving faith in men. Yet what was said before remains true: there is no spiritual conviction of the judgment except what arises from an apprehension of the spiritual beauty and glory of divine things. For, as has been observed, this apprehension or view tends to convince the mind of the truth of the gospel in two ways, either directly or indirectly. Having therefore already observed how it does this directly, I proceed now

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. To observe how a view of this divine glory convinces the mind of the truth of Christianity more indirectly.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] it does so in that the prejudices of the heart against the truth of divine things are removed by it, so that the mind lies open to the force of the reasons that are offered. The mind of man is naturally full of enmity against the doctrines of the gospel. This works against the arguments that prove their truth, and makes them lose their force on the mind. But when the divine excellence of Christian doctrines is shown to a person, this destroys that enmity, removes the prejudices, sanctifies the reason, and makes it open and free. This makes a vast difference to the force that arguments have to convince the mind. This explains the very different effect Christ’s miracles had in convincing the #emph[disciples] from what they had in convincing the #emph[scribes] and #emph[Pharisees.] It was not that the disciples had a stronger reason, or had their reason more cultivated; but their reason was sanctified, and those blinding prejudices which the #emph[scribes] and #emph[Pharisees] were under were removed by the sense they had of the excellence of Christ and his doctrine.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] it not only removes the hindrances of reason but positively helps reason. It makes even the speculative notions more lively. It assists and engages the attention of the mind to that kind of object, which gives it a clearer view of them and lets it see their mutual relations more clearly. The ideas themselves, which are otherwise dim and obscure, have a light cast on them by this means and are impressed with greater strength, so that the mind can better judge of them. In the same way, he who looks at the objects on the face of the earth when the light of the sun is cast on them is at a greater advantage to discern them in their true forms and mutual relations, and to see the evidences of divine wisdom and skill in their design, than he who sees them in dim starlight or twilight.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[What has been said may serve in some measure to show the nature of a spiritual conviction of the judgment, of the truth and reality of divine things, and so to distinguish truly gracious affections from others; for gracious affections are always accompanied by such a conviction of the judgment.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But before I leave this head, it will be necessary to note the ways in which some are deceived in this matter, and to take notice of several things that are sometimes taken for a spiritual and saving belief of the truth of the things of religion, which are in fact very different from it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. There is a degree of conviction of the truth of the great things of religion that arises from the common enlightenings of the Spirit of God. That livelier and more deeply felt apprehension of the things of religion, as to what is #emph[natural] in them, such as natural men have who are under awakenings and common illuminations, will give some degree of conviction of the truth of divine things beyond what they had before they were enlightened in this way. For by it they see the manifestations of the natural perfections of God, such as his greatness, power and fearful majesty, that are in the revelation made in the holy Scriptures and in the things exhibited in that revelation. This tends to convince the mind that this is the word of a great and terrible God. From the signs of God’s greatness and majesty in his word and works, of which they have a great sense through the common influence of the Spirit of God, they may have a much greater conviction that these are indeed the word and works of a very great invisible Being. And the lively apprehension of the greatness of God which natural men may have tends to give them a sense of the great guilt that sin against such a God brings, and of the dreadfulness of his wrath for sin. And this tends to make them believe more easily and fully the revelation the Scripture makes of another world, and of the extreme misery it threatens to inflict there on sinners. And so, from that sense of the great natural good there is in the things of religion, which is sometimes given in common illuminations, men may be more inclined to believe the truth of religion. Persons may have these things, and yet have no sense of the beauty and loveliness of the moral and holy excellence that is in the things of religion, and therefore no spiritual conviction of their truth. Yet such convictions are sometimes mistaken for saving convictions, and the affections flowing from them for saving affections.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The extraordinary impressions made on the imaginations of some persons, in the visions and immediate strong impulses and suggestions they have, as though they saw sights and had words spoken to them, may and often do produce a strong persuasion of the truth of invisible things. The general tendency of such things, in their final outcome, is to draw men away from the word of God, to make them reject the gospel, and to establish unbelief and atheism. Yet for the present they may, and often do, produce a confident persuasion of the truth of some things revealed in the Scriptures, though their confidence is founded on delusion and so is worth nothing. For instance, suppose some invisible agent immediately and strongly impresses on a person’s imagination the appearance of a bright light, and the glorious form of a person seated on a throne with great outward majesty and beauty, uttering some remarkable words with great force and energy. The person who is the subject of such an operation may from this be confident that there are invisible agents, spiritual beings, from what he has experienced, knowing that he himself had no hand in this extraordinary effect he has experienced. He may also be confident that this is Christ, whom he saw and heard speaking. And this may make him confident that there is a Christ, and that Christ reigns on a throne in heaven as he saw him; and he may be confident that the words he heard him speak are true, and so on. In the same way the lying miracles of the papists may for the present produce in the minds of the ignorant, deluded people a strong persuasion of the truth of many things declared in the New Testament. So when the images of Christ in popish churches are, on some extraordinary occasions, made by priestcraft to appear to the people as if they wept, shed fresh blood, moved, and uttered such and such words, the people may truly be persuaded that it is a miracle worked by Christ himself. From this they may be confident that there is a Christ, and that what they are told of his death and sufferings, resurrection, ascension and present government of the world is true; for they may look on this miracle as a certain evidence of all these things, and a kind of demonstration of them before their eyes. This may be the influence of these lying wonders for the present, though their general tendency is not to convince people that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh, but in the end to promote atheism. Even the dealings that #emph[Satan] has with witches, and their frequent experience of his immediate power, tend to convince them of the truth of some of the doctrines of religion, in particular the reality of an invisible world, or world of spirits, contrary to the doctrine of the #emph[Sadducees.] The general tendency of #emph[Satan]’s influences is delusion; but he may still mix some truth with his lies, so that his lies may not be so easily discovered.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[There are multitudes who are deluded with a counterfeit faith from impressions on their imagination, in the way just spoken of. They say they know that there is a God, for they have seen him; they know that Christ is the Son of God, for they have seen him in his glory; they know that Christ died for sinners, for they have seen him hanging on the cross, and his blood running from his wounds; they know there is a heaven and a hell, for they have seen the misery of the damned souls in hell and the glory of saints and angels in heaven (meaning some outward representations strongly impressed on their imagination). They know that the Scriptures are the word of God, and that such and such promises in particular are his word, for they have heard him speak them to them: they came to their minds suddenly and immediately from God, without their having any hand in it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Persons may seem to have their belief of the truth of the things of religion greatly increased when its foundation is only a persuasion they have received that they themselves have an interest, a share, in them. First, by some means or other, they take up a confidence that if there is a Christ and a heaven, they are theirs; and this prejudices them more in favor of the truth of these things. When they hear of the great and glorious things of religion, it is with this notion, that all these things belong to them; and so they easily become confident that they are true. They regard it as greatly to their advantage that they should be true. It is very obvious what a strong influence men’s self-interest and inclinations have on their judgments. While a natural man thinks that, if there is a heaven and a hell, the latter and not the former belongs to him, he will be hard to persuade that there is a heaven or hell. But when he comes to be persuaded that hell belongs only to other people and not to him, then he can easily allow the reality of hell, and cry out against others’ senselessness and folly in neglecting the means of escaping it. And being confident that he is a child of God, and that God has promised heaven to him, he may seem strong in the faith of its reality, and may have a great zeal against the unbelief that denies it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But I proceed to another distinguishing sign of gracious affections.]]
